  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct approach is to <b>sign into the instance directly and validate whether the recipe was configured correctly</b>. This allows you to examine log files (such as those stored in <b>/var/log/aws/opsworks/</b> or Chef logs), inspect the environment, and check any relevant configuration details firsthand. This direct access enables you to pinpoint script errors, missing dependencies, or misconfigurations immediately.</p><p>The following explains why the other options are not ideal:</p><ul><li><i>Activate AWS CloudTrail and review the OpsWorks logs for troubleshooting</i>: While CloudTrail provides a record of API calls made in your AWS environment, it does not contain the detailed operational logs or errors generated by recipe execution, making it insufficient for this purpose.</li><li><i>Enable AWS Config and inspect the OpsWorks logs to determine the problem</i>: AWS Config tracks changes to your AWS resources, not operational errors or log output from OpsWorks recipes, so it won’t help diagnose why a recipe failed.</li><li><i>Deregister the affected instance and examine the EC2 service logs</i>: Deregistering the instance could disrupt your stack and EC2 service logs will not give insight into the OpsWorks-specific recipe failure.</li></ul><p><b>Tip:</b> For OpsWorks troubleshooting, always check the instance's log files directly or through OpsWorks console's log view.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You have set up an OpsWorks stack utilizing Linux-based EC2 instances. After attempting to run a recipe, the execution fails. What is the most effective method to diagnose the cause of this failure?</p>",
